  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "If \\(w\\) is one of the imaginary roots of \\(x^3=1,\\) then \\((1-w+w^2)(1+w-w^2)\\) is equal to:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(4\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(w\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(w^2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(1\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "a",
    "value": "4"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1971-p22.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Because \\(w\\ne1\\) and \\(w^3=1,\\) we have \\(1+w+w^2=0.\\) Thus \\[ \\begin{gathered} 1-w+w^2=-2w,\\\\ 1+w-w^2=-2w^2. \\end{gathered} \\] Their product is \\(4w^3=4.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A.",
      "hints": [
        "Use \\(w^2+w+1=0\\)",
        "Replace \\(1+w^2\\) by \\(-w\\) and \\(1+w\\) by \\(-w^2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
